1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,960
Star, one five one, hash.

2
00:00:03,440 --> 00:00:06,400
Star, three three four, hash.

3
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:11,200
Across Africa, codes like these
open the door to people's money.

4
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:16,827
Sub-Saharan Africa now has more than one
billion registered mobile money accounts.

5
00:00:16,853 --> 00:00:18,080
And a few years ago,

6
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:23,440
the GSMA estimated that over ninety percent
of mobile money transactions in Africa

7
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:26,640
were still driven by one technology.

8
00:00:26,760 --> 00:00:29,040
Not apps. Not the internet.

9
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:31,280
Short codes that you dial.

10
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:37,040
In Nigeria, those codes became so important
that banks and phone networks argued for

11
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:39,840
four years over who should pay for them,

12
00:00:39,920 --> 00:00:44,000
a bill that grew to almost
three hundred billion naira.

13
00:00:44,080 --> 00:00:46,907
The technology is called USSD,

14
00:00:46,933 --> 00:00:51,480
and it was written into the
mobile phone standard in 1995.

15
00:00:51,520 --> 00:00:56,613
So how does a code from the nineties
still move so much of Africa's money?

16
00:00:56,667 --> 00:01:02,000
Let's find out. USSD stands for
Unstructured Supplementary Service

17
00:01:02,080 --> 00:01:06,600
Data. It's a mouthful, so here's
what it means in real life.

18
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:10,587
When you dial a code that starts
with a star and ends with a hash,

19
00:01:10,613 --> 00:01:15,160
and a little menu pops up
on your screen, that's USSD.

20
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:17,640
Check your balance. Buy a bundle.

21
00:01:17,680 --> 00:01:21,840
Send money. Press one, press reply, done.

22
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:27,800
USSD was standardised in 1995,
as part of GSM Phase 2,

23
00:01:27,840 --> 00:01:32,400
the set of rules written by the European
Telecommunications Standards Institute for

24
00:01:32,480 --> 00:01:36,600
the GSM mobile networks used
across most of the world.

25
00:01:36,640 --> 00:01:41,720
Those same GSM standards include
another code you can try right now.

26
00:01:41,760 --> 00:01:47,000
Dial star, hash, zero six,
hash, on almost any phone,

27
00:01:47,040 --> 00:01:51,960
even with no SIM card inside, and it
shows your phone's unique serial number,

28
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:57,720
called the IMEI. So what actually
happens when you press dial?

29
00:01:57,760 --> 00:02:00,680
Your code goes to the nearest cell tower.

30
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:03,880
From there, it goes to the
network's switching centre,

31
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:09,080
and then to the home register, the database
where your network keeps your details.

32
00:02:09,120 --> 00:02:12,680
The network then hands
it to a USSD gateway,

33
00:02:12,720 --> 00:02:17,333
which passes it to the computer of
whoever runs that code: your network,

34
00:02:17,387 --> 00:02:20,213
your bank, or a service like ours.

35
00:02:20,267 --> 00:02:26,000
The answer travels back the same way,
usually in a moment, and a menu appears.

36
00:02:26,080 --> 00:02:28,120
Two things make this special.

37
00:02:28,160 --> 00:02:30,520
First, it's a live session.

38
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:33,720
An SMS is stored and then delivered.

39
00:02:33,760 --> 00:02:38,040
A USSD session stays open
while you read and reply,

40
00:02:38,080 --> 00:02:41,040
a bit like a short
phone call made of text.

41
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:44,693
Second, it travels on the
network's signalling channels,

42
00:02:44,747 --> 00:02:48,400
the same lanes the network
uses to set up your calls.

43
00:02:48,480 --> 00:02:52,920
That's why USSD doesn't need data,
or an internet connection.

44
00:02:52,960 --> 00:02:55,480
Just an ordinary mobile signal.

45
00:02:55,520 --> 00:02:57,440
Of course, there are limits.

46
00:02:57,520 --> 00:03:03,080
Each USSD message can hold at most
one hundred and eighty-two characters.

47
00:03:03,360 --> 00:03:07,400
That's why the menus are so short,
and full of numbers to press.

48
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:09,680
And sessions don't last forever.

49
00:03:10,160 --> 00:03:14,880
The standard allows up to six hundred
seconds, but according to the ITU,

50
00:03:15,280 --> 00:03:20,040
networks typically allow about one hundred
and twenty seconds for a whole session.

51
00:03:20,320 --> 00:03:23,760
Take too long to find your PIN,
and you're back at the start.

52
00:03:24,080 --> 00:03:28,240
There are no pictures,
no logos, and no buttons to tap.

53
00:03:28,320 --> 00:03:33,120
Just text. But that simplicity
is exactly its superpower.

54
00:03:34,011 --> 00:03:38,571
Here's why. In 2024,
according to the GSMA,

55
00:03:38,651 --> 00:03:43,691
only about a quarter of people in
Sub-Saharan Africa used mobile internet.

56
00:03:43,771 --> 00:03:46,171
And the main problem isn't coverage.

57
00:03:46,291 --> 00:03:50,731
Only about ten percent of people in the
region lived outside mobile broadband

58
00:03:50,811 --> 00:03:54,891
coverage. A much bigger
group, sixty-five percent,

59
00:03:54,971 --> 00:03:59,051
lived where mobile internet was
available, but didn't use it.

60
00:03:59,131 --> 00:04:03,891
The reasons the GSMA points to include
the cost of smartphones and data,

61
00:04:03,931 --> 00:04:09,291
and digital skills. Smartphones
are growing fast, but in 2022,

62
00:04:09,331 --> 00:04:13,264
they were only about half of all
mobile connections in the region.

63
00:04:13,318 --> 00:04:16,211
The rest were basic and feature phones.

64
00:04:16,251 --> 00:04:18,931
USSD works on all of them.

65
00:04:18,971 --> 00:04:24,371
There's no app to download, no data bundle
to buy, and no storage space needed.

66
00:04:24,411 --> 00:04:27,891
If your phone can make a
call, it can dial a code.

67
00:04:27,931 --> 00:04:32,771
That's how a few short codes became
the front doors of whole money systems.

68
00:04:32,811 --> 00:04:38,704
In Zimbabwe, EcoCash simplified its
code to star, one five one, hash,

69
00:04:38,758 --> 00:04:44,224
back in 2014. In Kenya, M-Pesa
first ran on a SIM card menu,

70
00:04:44,278 --> 00:04:49,251
and in 2020 Safaricom put all
of its M-Pesa services on star,

71
00:04:49,291 --> 00:04:51,331
three three four, hash.

72
00:04:51,371 --> 00:04:57,091
In India, the national payments body
launched star, nine nine, hash, in 2012,

73
00:04:57,131 --> 00:04:59,638
so people could bank from any phone.

74
00:04:59,664 --> 00:05:01,811
And the numbers are huge.

75
00:05:01,851 --> 00:05:03,558
According to the GSMA,

76
00:05:03,584 --> 00:05:07,691
there were two point one billion registered
mobile money accounts in the world in

77
00:05:07,771 --> 00:05:13,131
2024, and more than one billion of
them were in Sub-Saharan Africa.

78
00:05:13,211 --> 00:05:18,411
That year, the region processed about one
point one trillion US dollars in mobile

79
00:05:18,851 --> 00:05:20,451
money. In South Africa,

80
00:05:20,491 --> 00:05:25,851
the 2022 census counted about two point
four million people who were born in another

81
00:05:26,331 --> 00:05:29,331
country. Many of them send support home.

82
00:05:29,371 --> 00:05:33,504
And many do it with cash,
from a phone with no data.

83
00:05:33,558 --> 00:05:37,251
A voucher, and a code they
can dial from any phone.

84
00:05:37,291 --> 00:05:39,931
That's exactly why we built Fluxr.

85
00:05:40,011 --> 00:05:42,731
This story is brought to you by Fluxr.

86
