94
00:06:14,411 --> 00:06:16,091
Now, back to the story.

95
00:06:17,048 --> 00:06:21,368
Because USSD has one big
question hanging over it.

96
00:06:21,688 --> 00:06:25,208
Who pays? Every session uses the network.

97
00:06:25,848 --> 00:06:30,168
In Nigeria, banks built
USSD codes for transfers,

98
00:06:30,208 --> 00:06:33,208
and the phone networks billed
the banks for the sessions.

99
00:06:33,528 --> 00:06:37,128
The banks didn't pay it all,
and the bill kept growing.

100
00:06:37,688 --> 00:06:43,208
By 2024, it was estimated at between
two hundred and fifty and three hundred

101
00:06:43,528 --> 00:06:48,088
billion naira. In March
2025, the regulator,

102
00:06:48,168 --> 00:06:51,768
the Nigerian Communications
Commission, set a price:

103
00:06:51,928 --> 00:06:56,648
six naira ninety-eight kobo for a banking
session of up to one hundred and twenty

104
00:06:56,808 --> 00:07:02,008
seconds. It also allowed the networks to
charge that fee to customers' airtime,

105
00:07:02,088 --> 00:07:03,128
with their consent.

106
00:07:03,768 --> 00:07:06,408
From June 2025, they did.

107
00:07:07,208 --> 00:07:09,288
In February 2026,

108
00:07:09,328 --> 00:07:14,888
the operators' association announced that
there was no outstanding USSD debt left.

109
00:07:15,208 --> 00:07:20,968
Nearly three hundred billion naira, built
up over four years, was finally cleared.

110
00:07:21,688 --> 00:07:22,688
The lesson?

111
00:07:22,888 --> 00:07:26,728
USSD looks simple,
but every session has a cost.

112
00:07:27,288 --> 00:07:32,328
Someone has to carry it: the bank,
the customer, or the service you're using.

113
00:07:33,208 --> 00:07:37,448
So is USSD dying? Smartphones are coming.

114
00:07:37,928 --> 00:07:42,248
The GSMA expects them to make up about
eighty-eight percent of connections in

115
00:07:42,328 --> 00:07:45,288
Sub-Saharan Africa by 2030.

116
00:07:45,368 --> 00:07:51,128
And apps can do things a code never
will: pictures, history, receipts,

117
00:07:51,208 --> 00:07:53,088
and much easier menus.

118
00:07:53,448 --> 00:07:55,728
But a code still has its place.

119
00:07:56,008 --> 00:07:57,288
When the data runs out.

120
00:07:57,608 --> 00:07:59,008
When the phone is old.

121
00:07:59,208 --> 00:08:03,448
When the person you're helping is a
grandmother who has never downloaded an app in

122
00:08:03,528 --> 00:08:04,528
her life.

123
00:08:04,968 --> 00:08:08,968
So the future probably isn't USSD or apps.

124
00:08:09,208 --> 00:08:14,408
It's both. An app for people who have
data, and a code for everyone else.

125
00:08:15,288 --> 00:08:20,088
Let's recap. USSD was
standardised in 1995.

126
00:08:20,728 --> 00:08:24,648
It opens a live text session on
the network's signalling channels,

127
00:08:24,728 --> 00:08:27,528
so it works on any phone, with no data.

128
00:08:28,248 --> 00:08:32,008
Each screen holds up to one
hundred and eighty-two characters,

129
00:08:32,048 --> 00:08:35,288
and networks usually allow
about two minutes per session.

130
00:08:36,008 --> 00:08:39,848
In a region where only a quarter
of people use mobile internet,

131
00:08:39,928 --> 00:08:43,608
that made USSD the front
door to mobile money,

132
00:08:43,688 --> 00:08:46,248
from EcoCash to M-Pesa.

133
00:08:46,328 --> 00:08:51,688
And in Nigeria, it showed that
even a simple code has a real cost.

134
00:08:51,768 --> 00:08:55,208
If you enjoyed this story,
subscribe for the next one.

135
00:08:55,248 --> 00:08:59,368
And if you're sending airtime and
data home, you know where to find us.

